Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.480
hi there, and thanks so much for joining us&nbsp;
today. My name is Bradley Knapp with IBM,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.200
and today the topic that we're going to&nbsp;
talk about is desktop virtualization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.600
and this is a topic that's gotten a whole lot&nbsp;
of press very recently because of some recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.280
announcements in the field, but I just want to&nbsp;
give a very brief overview of what is this desktop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:26.240
virtualization thing and how could it potentially&nbsp;
help you or your company. So, If we think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.760
desktop virtualization holistically, we've got to&nbsp;
go back in time, and really not that far in time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:39.120
or even to the present day, where we have a&nbsp;
person right that person pretty much no matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.720
what his job is there's going to be some level of&nbsp;
computing involved, right? So that guy is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.680
to have his laptop and he's going to be typing&nbsp;
away at the laptop, maybe he's got a desktop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.280
but computing is integrated into everything that&nbsp;
we do, and so if you think about that from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.880
business perspective, that means that businesses&nbsp;
have to buy, and issue, and secure thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.800
or tens or thousands or hundreds of thousands of&nbsp;
physical computing devices, and there's obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.600
some risk that comes along with that, right? If&nbsp;
you've got a laptop it can be stolen if you've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.840
a desktop you know they're anchored to the desk&nbsp;
they're not going to wander off on their own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.240
but you're leaving it unattended all the time. So&nbsp;
you've got a risk if you haven't properly secured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.880
it, someone can get in and get to that physical&nbsp;
machine, and do things they're not supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.640
that's on the information worker side, but&nbsp;
let's also think about a factory floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.400
or let's think about a hospital, or let's think&nbsp;
about a school where you've got to maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.280
all of these computing devices some of which&nbsp;
are in kind of rough environments, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.680
Kids are not the most gentle folks on the planet,&nbsp;
and factory production floors aren't either,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.360
and so computers are for better or for worse&nbsp;
somewhat delicate devices. So we've got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.560
take care of them, and in addition to that&nbsp;
you've got the security headache of securing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.600
not just the physical machines, but also securing&nbsp;
all of the user accounts for them. Securing them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:07.120
against penetration against theft against loss,&nbsp;
and so we have to think a little bit about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:13.040
is there a better way? Is there a way where we can&nbsp;
centralize more so we don't have to buy as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.640
hardware? Or if we do have to buy as much hardware&nbsp;
we can buy less expensive hardware? So can we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:24.640
centralize it, can we lower our costs, and can we&nbsp;
do it in a more secure manner? When somebody's got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.080
an individual laptop they can install software&nbsp;
on it, generally speaking, and there's always a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.440
possibility that that software can cause some&nbsp;
sort of a security vulnerability. There have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:41.360
been lots of great published presentations as&nbsp;
of late about software that gets installed onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:48.320
a local machine that inadvertently causes much&nbsp;
greater security problems. So how do we fix these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:55.840
problems? And one way, certainly not the only&nbsp;
way, but one way is to do desktop virtualization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.120
where the desktop rather than running on the local&nbsp;
machine running, on that physical machine itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:07.760
we are going to run it remotely. We're going&nbsp;
to run it in the cloud, and so instead of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:14.960
physical high-powered laptop. Let’s imagine&nbsp;
that we've got 10 architects in an architect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.320
firm. Architects need high-end machines, right?&nbsp;
They do lots of processing, lots of cad work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.040
they need very powerful processors, they need&nbsp;
lots of ram, they need graphics acceleration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.720
So to buy each one of them a very powerful laptop&nbsp;
which, by the way is not terribly portable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.920
we're going to have to spend a lot of money.&nbsp;
Is there an alternative? There is, desktop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:46.080
virtualization. So let us imagine a physical&nbsp;
server, cloud server, sitting in a data center.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.440
We'll call that server. There we&nbsp;
go. Now this server is 20, or 30,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.960
or 50 times more powerful from a compute&nbsp;
performance perspective than this laptop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.480
but it can perform all of the same functions.&nbsp;
You can put the same graphics accelerators in it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.800
You can stuff them full of ram so that it&nbsp;
can get equivalent or better performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:13.680
but it's in a data center. How useful is that?&nbsp;
Well the answer is you take this server and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:22.400
you divide it up into virtual machines that&nbsp;
each machine is a virtual desktop. So you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:29.120
a virtual desktop for each one of your&nbsp;
individual users, and you get to stack them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:36.400
on top of this server. So one piece of physical&nbsp;
infrastructure can host 4, or 10, or 20, or 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:42.960
concurrent desktop sessions. Well, why is this&nbsp;
good? well let's imagine a 24-hour workplace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:50.640
notice I said concurrent desktop sessions before,&nbsp;
so if you've got users that are working 24/7/365,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:56.160
like it's say a hospital, rather than having&nbsp;
to have a laptop for each one of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:02.160
they just get a session. Well if you've got a&nbsp;
thousand employees and they work about 500 during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:08.240
the day and about 500 at night you only have to&nbsp;
size your infrastructure to run for 500 people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.400
plus a little bit of overhead. So you're going&nbsp;
to save money because you're going to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:19.280
density. You're also going to be able to take&nbsp;
all of these big expensive enterprise benefits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.080
but you only have to buy half as many, right?&nbsp;
Because you are serving multiple users out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:30.160
that same physical machine. Now let's go&nbsp;
back to our hospital for a moment here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.640
hospital, you've got a nurse who's walking around&nbsp;
she's got a certain number of patients, four,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:40.880
six, eight, patients, right? And maybe each&nbsp;
room in that hospital has its own computer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.680
right? So you've got a physical device there,&nbsp;
it's not a powerful device, because all it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:52.480
is host these virtual sessions up here. Well&nbsp;
as that nurse goes from room, to room, to room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:57.600
she doesn't want to lose whatever she's working&nbsp;
on. She's got all of the stuff that she's already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.840
doing, plus what she's going to be doing in that&nbsp;
next room. This is another place where virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.880
desktops are really cool, right? so let's call&nbsp;
this this box right here, this is going to be her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:13.680
session. This is her concurrent session. So she's&nbsp;
in room one, and she logs in on that machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:19.520
to her user session, and so she's got her email&nbsp;
running. She's got the the patient records open.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:25.280
She's got lab records open, maybe some x-rays,&nbsp;
something like that, and so that is all running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:31.760
in this session that is unique to her. Now she&nbsp;
gets done in that room and it's time for her to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:38.800
physically go over to room number two. Now all she&nbsp;
has to do is disconnect this session right here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:44.480
so we'll put an x on it, and reconnect from the&nbsp;
computer in room number two, but the session is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:50.640
stateful, it's still live, and so nothing has&nbsp;
changed on the screen that is displayed to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.960
even though she's now on a different&nbsp;
physical device. she's logged in on a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:02.000
new machine now, and she can do that as she goes&nbsp;
to room three, and four, and so on, and so forth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:08.640
because the session is running on the server. It's&nbsp;
running in the cloud. She doesn't ever have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.720
log off, and log back on again, and rebuild the&nbsp;
machine, like if you were going in an old school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.040
environment where you logged off a machine and&nbsp;
then you would have to reload it off the domain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.000
Because it the actual desktop itself,&nbsp;
all of the applications on the desktop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.920
are residing on that server, all she's doing is&nbsp;
accessing her session. So not only do you get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:34.800
increased density, which is a great thing, lowers&nbsp;
cost, you also get this concurrency of session,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.040
which is great for your employees because they&nbsp;
aren't constantly logging out and logging in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:44.640
and logging out and logging, in and moving from&nbsp;
place, to place, to place. Another place that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.520
virtual desktops are great, let's imagine&nbsp;
something like a call center environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.280
where you don't have assigned seating, right?&nbsp;
The employees come in they sit down wherever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:59.120
they need to be, they log in, and they start&nbsp;
work. Well because these sessions reside on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.400
the server whenever you bring them up, they can&nbsp;
be brought up onto any client, right? And so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:10.000
ability to bring up any client is important,&nbsp;
bring up on any clients is very important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:14.400
because now you don't have to worry about the&nbsp;
problems of assigned sitting, and this person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:19.440
can only sit in this cube, because it's the only&nbsp;
one that is authorized to bring up their session.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:26.240
So again the increased flexibility that you get&nbsp;
is just fantastic. Mow there's one other really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:32.160
neat part about virtual desktops that's very&nbsp;
specific to security that I do want to touch on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:38.480
So let us imagine for a moment a scenario&nbsp;
where you've got security information, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:44.480
Information that needs to be heavily protected&nbsp;
against loss, and so we've got our guy over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:49.600
He's got his laptop. He's working at home, but&nbsp;
he needs to get into this secured information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:56.640
and for various internal reasons he is not allowed&nbsp;
to access that information and store it on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:05.040
local machine. What he can do is, he can create a&nbsp;
session out of his local machine into our server,

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:12.400
and he can open a virtual desktop on his physical&nbsp;
desktop. It runs in a window just like any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:18.640
window. So he can create a session that is going&nbsp;
to display to him that secured information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:23.680
but it's going to display it in a secured way,&nbsp;
right? Because he's not caching it locally on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:28.880
his machine, it can't be stolen off of it. All&nbsp;
he's seeing is an image of what he's looking at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:34.960
and that image is delivered via the secure&nbsp;
server. So again helping to ensure security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:39.040
helping to ensure access control, making sure&nbsp;
that only the right people are getting to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:44.320
documents that they need to get to. So that's&nbsp;
a really brief overview of virtual desktops.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:49.840
Hopefully it was useful and if you&nbsp;
have any questions, just let us know.

